## Proof

Define
$$g(z) = f(z) - e^{z}\sin 2z.$$

Since $f$ is entire and $z \mapsto e^{z}\sin 2z$ is entire, $g$ is entire. The hypothesis gives
$$|g(z)| < 4 \quad \text{for all } z \in \mathbb{C}.$$

Thus $g$ is a bounded entire function. By **Liouville's theorem**, $g$ is constant: there exists $c \in \mathbb{C}$ with $|c|<4$ such that $g(z)=c$ for all $z$.

Now use the initial condition $f(0)=1$:
$$f(0) = g(0) + e^{0}\sin 0 = c + 1\cdot 0 = c.$$

Hence $c = 1$ (which satisfies $|c|=1<4$, consistent with the bound). Therefore
$$f(z) = 1 + e^{z}\sin 2z \quad \text{for all } z\in\mathbb{C}.$$

Evaluating at $z=1$:
$$f(1) = 1 + e^{1}\sin 2 = 1 + e\sin 2.$$

$$\boxed{f(1) = 1 + e\sin 2}$$

